1
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:01,820
April fifth.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,140
Rent day.

3
00:00:03,540 --> 00:00:05,780
Meera counts out five thousand rupees and

4
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:07,140
hands it to the landlord.

5
00:00:07,709 --> 00:00:10,349
Last time, every rupee that left the galla

6
00:00:10,509 --> 00:00:13,389
came back as something — a cart, some tea

7
00:00:13,469 --> 00:00:14,029
and sugar.

8
00:00:14,509 --> 00:00:15,509
But this time?

9
00:00:15,789 --> 00:00:17,389
No new asset arrives.

10
00:00:17,469 --> 00:00:18,989
No debt gets smaller.

11
00:00:19,069 --> 00:00:20,269
Nothing comes back.

12
00:00:21,555 --> 00:00:22,902
The scale tips.

13
00:00:23,182 --> 00:00:25,182
So what's on the other side?

14
00:00:31,391 --> 00:00:33,551
First, last time's answers.

15
00:00:33,871 --> 00:00:34,471
One.

16
00:00:34,751 --> 00:00:37,511
After Gopal's delivery, the equation reads:

17
00:00:37,711 --> 00:00:39,551
eighty-eight thousand equals thirty-eight

18
00:00:39,711 --> 00:00:42,031
thousand, plus fifty thousand.

19
00:00:42,711 --> 00:00:43,231
Two.

20
00:00:43,511 --> 00:00:45,511
Buying the cart for cash doesn't move the

21
00:00:45,591 --> 00:00:46,311
scale.

22
00:00:46,471 --> 00:00:49,031
Cash goes down, the cart comes in — a swap

23
00:00:49,071 --> 00:00:50,151
on the left.

24
00:00:50,831 --> 00:00:51,391
Three.

25
00:00:51,631 --> 00:00:53,871
Paying back part of Ravi Mama's loan?

26
00:00:54,111 --> 00:00:57,071
Cash goes down, and so does what Meera owes.

27
00:00:57,191 --> 00:00:59,231
Both sides go down together, and the scale

28
00:00:59,311 --> 00:01:00,351
stays level.

29
00:01:00,931 --> 00:01:02,931
And paying back a loan is not an expense

30
00:01:03,251 --> 00:01:04,931
— the debt just got smaller.

31
00:01:05,979 --> 00:01:07,499
So, back to rent day.

32
00:01:07,979 --> 00:01:08,819
Freeze.

33
00:01:09,099 --> 00:01:10,699
Which two things changed?

34
00:01:12,655 --> 00:01:14,255
The first one is easy.

35
00:01:14,415 --> 00:01:16,495
Cash went down by five thousand.

36
00:01:16,575 --> 00:01:17,695
But the second?

37
00:01:17,775 --> 00:01:20,415
Ravi Mama is still owed thirty thousand.

38
00:01:20,455 --> 00:01:22,895
Gopal is still owed eight thousand.

39
00:01:23,095 --> 00:01:24,815
Nobody's debt got smaller.

40
00:01:25,455 --> 00:01:27,655
So the change has to land on the only thing

41
00:01:27,735 --> 00:01:30,055
left — Meera's own share.

42
00:01:30,175 --> 00:01:30,855
Equity.

43
00:01:31,389 --> 00:01:33,309
Let's unfold Meera's tag.

44
00:01:33,549 --> 00:01:36,589
It opens into a card — the equity card —

45
00:01:36,669 --> 00:01:37,869
with two pockets.

46
00:01:38,609 --> 00:01:41,169
Capital: the fifty thousand Meera put in.

47
00:01:41,249 --> 00:01:43,169
And a new, empty pocket.

48
00:01:43,709 --> 00:01:45,829
The rent comes out of that new pocket.

49
00:01:45,909 --> 00:01:48,709
For now, it reads minus five thousand.

50
00:01:49,262 --> 00:01:51,582
Eighty-three thousand equals thirty-eight

51
00:01:51,742 --> 00:01:54,622
thousand, plus forty-five thousand.

52
00:01:54,662 --> 00:01:55,822
The scale settles.

53
00:01:56,902 --> 00:01:59,142
Money that is used up to run the business,

54
00:01:59,222 --> 00:02:01,942
gone for good, is called an expense.

55
00:02:02,782 --> 00:02:05,342
An expense shrinks the owner's share.

56
00:02:06,759 --> 00:02:08,159
Now the fun part.

57
00:02:08,359 --> 00:02:10,159
Meera opens for business.

58
00:02:10,359 --> 00:02:12,679
Raju, her new helper, keeps the glasses

59
00:02:12,759 --> 00:02:13,319
moving.

60
00:02:13,559 --> 00:02:16,759
Two weeks of chai — glass after glass after

61
00:02:16,919 --> 00:02:17,639
glass.

62
00:02:17,997 --> 00:02:20,717
By April fifteenth, the galla has collected

63
00:02:20,797 --> 00:02:23,197
eighteen thousand rupees from sales.

64
00:02:23,357 --> 00:02:25,597
Cash goes up by eighteen thousand.

65
00:02:25,677 --> 00:02:27,037
Meera, your turn.

66
00:02:27,117 --> 00:02:28,397
Where's the second change?

67
00:02:30,446 --> 00:02:32,606
She didn't borrow it, so there's no new

68
00:02:32,686 --> 00:02:33,406
debt.

69
00:02:33,486 --> 00:02:36,206
She didn't add her savings, so capital stays

70
00:02:36,286 --> 00:02:37,566
at fifty thousand.

71
00:02:37,726 --> 00:02:38,766
She earned it.

72
00:02:38,886 --> 00:02:41,326
So it goes into the new pocket — right beside

73
00:02:41,406 --> 00:02:42,126
the rent.

74
00:02:42,596 --> 00:02:45,316
One lakh one thousand equals thirty-eight

75
00:02:45,476 --> 00:02:48,356
thousand, plus sixty-three thousand.

76
00:02:49,076 --> 00:02:50,876
Money the business earns by selling what

77
00:02:50,956 --> 00:02:53,276
it sells is called revenue.

78
00:02:53,956 --> 00:02:56,276
Revenue grows the owner's share.

79
00:02:57,531 --> 00:02:59,771
Now peek inside that new pocket.

80
00:03:00,011 --> 00:03:02,251
Eighteen thousand came in as revenue.

81
00:03:02,331 --> 00:03:04,731
Five thousand went out as an expense.

82
00:03:04,811 --> 00:03:05,691
What's left?

83
00:03:07,455 --> 00:03:09,055
Thirteen thousand rupees.

84
00:03:09,255 --> 00:03:13,375
That's profit, so far — revenue, minus expenses.

85
00:03:14,315 --> 00:03:15,995
Now the pocket has a name.

86
00:03:16,855 --> 00:03:18,295
And look where it lives.

87
00:03:18,495 --> 00:03:21,535
Inside equity, right next to Meera's capital.

88
00:03:22,275 --> 00:03:24,955
Ravi Mama and Gopal are owed fixed amounts.

89
00:03:25,155 --> 00:03:27,475
But every rupee of profit belongs to the

90
00:03:27,555 --> 00:03:28,195
owner.

91
00:03:28,746 --> 00:03:31,266
So our equation gets a little longer.

92
00:03:31,546 --> 00:03:34,506
Assets equal liabilities, plus capital,

93
00:03:34,626 --> 00:03:36,826
plus revenue, minus expenses.

94
00:03:37,826 --> 00:03:38,546
Same scale.

95
00:03:38,706 --> 00:03:40,866
We just looked inside Meera's share.

96
00:03:42,113 --> 00:03:44,753
Now, here's the trap most new owners fall

97
00:03:44,913 --> 00:03:45,153
into.

98
00:03:45,713 --> 00:03:49,073
Money left the galla, so it must be an expense.

99
00:03:49,596 --> 00:03:50,116
Nope.

100
00:03:50,316 --> 00:03:51,356
Remember the cart?

101
00:03:51,596 --> 00:03:53,436
Thirty-six thousand rupees left the galla

102
00:03:53,516 --> 00:03:54,476
for that too.

103
00:03:54,556 --> 00:03:56,076
So ask one question.

104
00:03:56,716 --> 00:03:58,796
Is it used up — gone for good?

105
00:03:59,036 --> 00:03:59,756
The rent?

106
00:03:59,996 --> 00:04:00,516
Yes.

107
00:04:00,716 --> 00:04:03,436
April is paid for, and May will need more.

108
00:04:03,516 --> 00:04:04,236
The cart?

109
00:04:04,476 --> 00:04:05,036
No.

110
00:04:05,076 --> 00:04:06,956
It's still standing right there, making

111
00:04:07,036 --> 00:04:07,756
chai.

112
00:04:08,376 --> 00:04:09,536
That's an asset.

113
00:04:10,784 --> 00:04:11,624
Your turn.

114
00:04:11,904 --> 00:04:14,144
Just imagine this — it's not in Meera's

115
00:04:14,224 --> 00:04:14,824
books.

116
00:04:15,104 --> 00:04:17,264
Meera pays three hundred rupees for a gas

117
00:04:17,344 --> 00:04:18,024
refill.

118
00:04:18,304 --> 00:04:20,064
Which two things changed?

119
00:04:23,231 --> 00:04:25,551
Cash, down three hundred.

120
00:04:25,591 --> 00:04:28,271
And the profit pocket, down three hundred.

121
00:04:28,751 --> 00:04:31,391
The gas burns away making chai — it's used

122
00:04:31,471 --> 00:04:31,711
up.

123
00:04:32,031 --> 00:04:32,991
An expense.

124
00:04:33,557 --> 00:04:34,397
One more.

125
00:04:34,677 --> 00:04:37,317
Meera buys a second kettle for fifteen hundred

126
00:04:37,397 --> 00:04:38,157
rupees.

127
00:04:38,437 --> 00:04:40,117
Which two things changed?

128
00:04:43,287 --> 00:04:45,687
Cash, down fifteen hundred.

129
00:04:45,727 --> 00:04:48,407
And Equipment, up fifteen hundred — a swap

130
00:04:48,447 --> 00:04:49,367
on the left.

131
00:04:49,847 --> 00:04:51,607
The profit pocket doesn't move.

132
00:04:51,647 --> 00:04:53,927
The kettle stays, and keeps making chai

133
00:04:54,007 --> 00:04:55,127
for years.

134
00:04:55,207 --> 00:04:56,167
An asset.

135
00:04:57,422 --> 00:05:00,182
So — Meera's share has two pockets.

136
00:05:00,382 --> 00:05:02,422
Revenue fills the profit pocket.

137
00:05:02,622 --> 00:05:04,302
Expenses drain it.

138
00:05:04,342 --> 00:05:06,142
And profit is the difference.

139
00:05:07,274 --> 00:05:07,994
Your turn.

140
00:05:08,154 --> 00:05:09,154
Three quick ones.

141
00:05:09,354 --> 00:05:09,834
One.

142
00:05:10,074 --> 00:05:12,074
Is the thirty-six-thousand-rupee cart an

143
00:05:12,154 --> 00:05:12,994
expense?

144
00:05:13,274 --> 00:05:13,914
Two.

145
00:05:13,994 --> 00:05:16,154
What's Meera's profit after the rent and

146
00:05:16,234 --> 00:05:17,994
the first two weeks of sales?

147
00:05:18,074 --> 00:05:18,794
Three.

148
00:05:18,874 --> 00:05:20,794
Which side of the scale does profit live

149
00:05:20,874 --> 00:05:21,514
on?

150
00:05:21,754 --> 00:05:23,674
Answers at the start of the next lesson.

151
00:05:25,085 --> 00:05:27,485
Next time, the office next door takes chai

152
00:05:27,565 --> 00:05:30,285
for the whole floor, every single day.

153
00:05:30,525 --> 00:05:31,525
Just one catch.

154
00:05:31,725 --> 00:05:32,765
They pay later.
